  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ensuring Access to Emergency and Essential Health Services for Afghan Returnees, Refugees, Underserved Migrant Populations, and Host Communities &#x2013; Year 3</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Afghanistan is facing an unprecedented humanitarian crisis with a very real risk of systemic collapse and human catastrophe. In addition to unimaginable human costs, this humanitarian crisis is reversing many of the gains of the last 20 years, including around women&#x2019;s rights. The 2023 Humanitarian Needs Overview (HNO) states that a total of 28.3 million people (two thirds of the population) need humanitarian assistance to survive. The curtailment of Afghan women&#x2019;s enjoyment of their rights is uniquely severe, with women&#x2019;s access to secondary and tertiary education completely restricted as well as women&#x2019;s engagement in the delivery of humanitarian aid through non-governmental organizations (NGOs) and the UN. 
 
The objective of this project is to ensure the continuity of a comprehensive and data-driven response for vulnerable migrants in Afghanistan by addressing the humanitarian, protection, and health needs of undocumented returnees at the borders and identifying the longer-term needs of returnees and internally displaced persons (IDPs) in key targeted provinces.</narrative>
  </description>
